  "observations": [
    "Actual validated resource inventories plus four coherent preference digests exceeded the old shared metadata limit; real production Profile.prepare refused at profile.json write. This is a measured selected-profile capacity defect, not proof of full release closure.",
    "The production fix enlarges only profile.json reads and writes. Default control/selection metadata and stricter preference limits, descriptor validation, resource hashes, locking, managed-child ownership, atomic selection and Unknown reconciliation are unchanged. Existing Quint model is unchanged.",
    "All original model and Profile/CLI/lifecycle/fault cases plus new large-resource and hard-bound rejection cases pass. Sampled safety and positive witnesses do not prove filesystem or native GUI behavior.",
    "The first repeat correctly stopped at the activation-free bus guard: the new module facade had shadowed the host class instead of replacing the original module binding used by the inherited session constructor. The one-line fixture correction targets host.original; all actual bus-peer/socket/audio/system-bus and timeout guards remain mandatory. Failed cleanup with unexpected children remains a failed result even though the outer host removed them.",
    "Corrected real preparation passes under the same five-second worker boundary; measured and persisted manifests are byte-equal, preference baseline digests match the failed trial, and cleanup has no unexpected or remaining descendants. Neither component is launched by this phase."
  ],
  "missingObservations": [
    "Actual current candidate guidance dismissal and later host failure/absence",
    "Actual owned editable GTK draft content through sequential failure/rollback/predecessor checkpoints",
    "Standalone documented offline rollback and usable installed Omarchy predecessor with source-backed retained effective preference observation",
    "Independent original offline-recovery verdict, complete reproducible release dependency closure, applicable wider AT/IME and main-session deployment"
